 "literal": "Those who live by ploughing and eating are indeed those who live; all others, having worshipped and eaten, are those who go behind.",
 "translation": "Only those who live by tilling the soil and eating their own food truly live; all others must bow down to them, eat, and follow in their wake.",
 "modern": "உழுது பயிர் செய்து அதனால் வரும் உணவை உண்டு வாழ்பவர்களே உண்மையாக வாழ்பவர்கள் ஆவர்; மற்றவர்கள் அனைவரும் பிறரைத் தொழுது, அவர்கள் தரும் உணவை உண்டு, அவர்களுக்குப் பின்னே செல்பவர்கள் ஆவர்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet highlights the supreme importance and independence of agriculture. The poet asserts that farmers, who till the soil and feed themselves, are the only ones who lead a truly independent and dignified life. All other people, regardless of their status, depend on the food produced by farmers. Consequently, they must bow to the producers of food, receive sustenance from them, and follow them as dependents.",
